abstract: >-
  DTree Explorer shows the incremental encoding of directed trees by DTree at
  work. DTree places every node in two linear projections Q1 and Q2; node u
  reaches node v if and only if u precedes v in both, and inserting a node
  adds two positions without moving any other node. The Explorer builds a
  tree edge by edge and draws it in three views: the tree, the plane in which
  each node sits at its rank in Q1 and Q2, and the projections with the
  positions the library stores. Clicking nodes shows what reaches a node and
  what it reaches, and answers whether one node reaches another with both
  comparisons. Since version 1.3.0 the Explorer also shows EulerTree, which
  keeps Q1 alone with a closing cell for every node with children (the Euler
  tour), and lets the user choose rational or integer positions. The examples include those of the chapter "Topology and
  Structure of Directed Trees: An Incremental Encoding", among them the
  generalized insertion before or after a sibling (operators "s > c" and
  "s < c"). Every tree is computed by the published library triordinal-dtree (formerly dtree-encoding):
  locally with the installed package (pip install dtree-explorer), or in the
  browser with Pyodide.
